# {Project Name} — Development Rules

## General

- Be concise. Don't explain what you're about to do — just do it.
- Don't add features, refactor code, or make improvements beyond what was asked.
- Don't add comments, docstrings, or type annotations to code you didn't change.
- Don't create helpers or abstractions for one-time operations.
- Don't design for hypothetical future requirements.
- Three similar lines of code is better than a premature abstraction.

## Code Changes

- Read code before modifying it. Understand what exists before suggesting changes.
- Prefer editing existing files over creating new ones.
- Don't add error handling for scenarios that can't happen.
- Don't add backwards-compatibility shims — just change the code.
- Only validate at system boundaries (user input, external APIs). Trust internal code.

## Git

- Use `/commit-push` for all commits.
- Don't commit unless explicitly asked.
- Don't push unless explicitly asked.
- Never use `git add -A` or `git add .` — stage specific files.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, or binaries.

## Testing

- Write table-driven tests with `t.Run` for subtests.
- Use `t.Helper()` in test helpers.
- Test names: `TestFunctionName`, `TestType_Method`, or `TestFunctionName_condition`.
- Run tests before considering work done.

## Go

- Run `/go` before submitting Go changes for review.
- Follow Effective Go, Practical Go, and standard library conventions.
- `cmd/` for binaries, `internal/` for private packages.
- Standard library imports first, blank line, then third-party, then internal.

